---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Spousal Support FAQ | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'Common questions about spousal support: who pays, how long, taxes, modification, and termination.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Spousal Support FAQ</h1>

  <h2>Who pays spousal support?</h2>
  <p>The higher-earning spouse, typically. Gender doesn't matter under modern statutes &mdash; either spouse can pay or receive based on relative incomes and circumstances.</p>

  <h2>How long does spousal support last?</h2>
  <p>Varies widely. Short marriages (under 5 years) often get little or no permanent support. Marriages over 20 years can lead to indefinite support, especially with significant income gaps. State guidelines often tie duration to a percentage of marriage length.</p>

  <h2>Is spousal support taxable?</h2>
  <p>For divorces finalized AFTER 12/31/2018: spousal support is NOT deductible to the payer and NOT taxable income to the recipient (federal). For divorces finalized BEFORE that date: opposite treatment under the old rules, unless modified post-2018.</p>

  <h2>Can spousal support be modified?</h2>
  <p>Yes, with a showing of material change in circumstances. Job loss, retirement, significant income changes, or remarriage typically trigger modification eligibility. Some agreements specify whether support is modifiable.</p>

  <h2>Does remarriage end spousal support?</h2>
  <p>Usually yes for the recipient's remarriage. Many state statutes automatically terminate spousal support on the recipient's remarriage. Cohabitation may also terminate or reduce support in some states.</p>

  <h2>What if my ex hides income?</h2>
  <p>Discovery during divorce (subpoenas, forensic accounting) addresses this. Most states have processes for income imputation if a spouse voluntarily reduces income to avoid support.</p>

  <h2>Do prenups override the formula?</h2>
  <p>Usually yes, if properly executed. Most states enforce prenuptial agreements that waive or limit spousal support, with exceptions for unconscionability or lack of disclosure.</p>

  <h2>What about child support?</h2>
  <p>Spousal support and child support are calculated separately but interact. Child support usually takes priority. Some state formulas calculate them together to avoid double-counting income.</p>

  <h2>Can the judge ignore the formula?</h2>
  <p>In most states, yes &mdash; especially for permanent support. Formula guidelines are starting points, not binding rules. Judges can deviate with documented reasoning.</p>
</BaseLayout>
